[[build-lifecycle]]
=== Process lifecycle

[abstract]
A run creates processes, a private directory and shared memory, and must
remove all of them however it ends: normally, by error, by signal, or by
being killed outright. This section specifies how a run starts its pools,
how it knows they are ready, how it stops them, and what it guarantees
about cleanup and exit status.

**Startup.** Before it starts any pool, the nexus:

. Creates the *run directory*, a fresh directory under `/tmp` readable only
  by the user, and writes an owner record into it (the nexus's pid, process
  start time, boot id and pid namespace). It always lives under `/tmp` so a
  later run can find it; the owner record tells a dead run from a live one.
. Removes the run directories and shared memory of earlier runs whose owner
  is dead and whose pools have all ended.
. Creates the run's shared memory: the first volume and the stream registry
  (<<build-shm>>).
. Installs its signal handlers.

It then starts each pool the request needs (<<build-overview>>):

. It starts a *pin*: a tiny process that leads a new process group, ignores
  termination signals, and exits only when the nexus closes a pipe to it.
  The pin holds the group's id, so the id cannot be reissued to an
  unrelated process while the nexus may still signal the group.
. It records the group in the run directory, then starts the pool inside
  that group. The pool's command line is the manifest's command for it
  followed by three arguments: its socket path, the run directory, and the
  shared-memory base name. The pool inherits the nexus's standard input,
  output and error unchanged.
. It pings each pool on its socket until one answers with a ping packet
  (<<build-packets>>). Retries back off exponentially. If the pool process
  exits first, startup fails at once and reports its exit status.

**The lifeline.** The nexus holds the only write end of a pipe; every pool
receives the read end. The kernel closes the write end when the nexus exits
for any reason, including `SIGKILL`, so a pool always learns that its nexus
is gone. It then ends its own process group (`SIGTERM`, 200 ms, `SIGKILL`)
and starts a detached *sweeper* that removes the run's shared memory and
directory once every recorded group has ended. Without the lifeline, a
killed nexus would leave pools and shared memory behind indefinitely.

**Shutdown.** At the end of a run, or on `SIGINT`, `SIGTERM` or `SIGHUP`,
the nexus stops its pools: `SIGTERM` to every pool group, up to 200 ms for
the pool processes to exit, then `SIGKILL` to every group. It then removes
the names of the shared-memory objects and the run directory. It removes
names but never unmaps memory at exit, because a background thread may
still be reading it. When a pool process exits on its own, the nexus kills
the rest of its group, so a pool's worker processes never outlive it.

**Cleanup on every path:**

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| How the run ends | Who removes shared memory and the run directory
| Normal exit, error, `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM`/`SIGHUP` | The nexus, before it exits.
| Crash signal (`SIGSEGV`, `SIGBUS`, `SIGFPE`, `SIGABRT`) | The nexus's crash handler removes the shared memory, then the signal takes its default action.
| Internal error (panic) | The nexus kills its pool groups and removes what it registered before exiting.
| `SIGKILL` or power loss of the nexus | Each pool's sweeper, after its lifeline closes; failing that, the next run's startup sweep.
|===

A long-running daemon additionally recovers from a pool crash by stopping
every pool, waiting for running requests, discarding the shared memory and
starting fresh; that is specified with the daemon in <<eco-serving>>.

**Exit status** of a one-shot run:

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Status | Meaning
| 0 | Success.
| 1 | The call failed: an error raised by user code, a pool that died, an argument that could not be read, or a runtime error.
| 2 | Usage error: an unknown command or option.
| 70 | Internal error: an invariant inside Morloc itself was broken (a panic). Only a panic exits with 70.
| 128 + _N_ | Ended by signal _N_ (130 for `SIGINT`, 143 for `SIGTERM`).
| 141 | Standard output was a closed pipe (128 + `SIGPIPE`), as when the output is piped to `head`.
|===

